# The runtime the host lends

A loaded language service ships only its own code. It carries no Effect and no service contract of its own. It borrows the host's, so the capability the host provides and the one the service asks for are the same instance rather than two copies that never meet. This module is the boundary where the host lends that runtime.

The host writes its live modules to a global the moment it starts. A service built to read that global, instead of importing the runtime by name, then runs on the host's exact instances. This is the literal form of the rule that the host owns the runtime, made mechanical: one Effect and one contract, shared across the load.

## The contract

Three names fix the boundary. `runtimeKey` is the global property the host writes and a service reads. `runtimeVersion` marks the shape of what is shared, so a service built against a newer host can tell. `runtimeSpecifiers` is the exact set of module specifiers the bridge covers — the imports a service resolves from the host rather than from its own files.

The set is deliberately small. It holds only what must be one shared instance: `effect`, because the host runs the service's `plugins` in its own runtime, and the `LanguageService` contract, because the capabilities and the service brand are keyed by it. Everything else a service needs — Volar's machinery, the `URI` type — it bundles, because those cross the boundary as plain data, not as identities.

The TypeScript compiler is not in the set. A service that needs a compiler asks the `TypescriptSdk` capability for one, and only when a project needs it. So the host lends no compiler it might never use, and a workspace with no TypeScript 6 installed still loads its services. The second version of the runtime is the one without TypeScript.

=>

export const runtimeKey = '__loomRuntime'

export const runtimeVersion = '2'

export const runtimeSpecifiers: ReadonlyArray<string> = [
  'effect',
  '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService',
]

## Lending the runtime

`installHostRuntime` writes the host's live modules to the global, once, before the first service loads. Effect and the service contract are the host's own imports, inlined in its bundle, so the host needs nothing handed in to lend them.

=>

import * as EffectNS from 'effect'
import * as LanguageServiceNS from './LanguageService'

interface HostRuntime {
  readonly version: string
  readonly modules: Record<string, unknown>
}

export const installHostRuntime = (): void => {
  ;(globalThis as Record<string, unknown>)[runtimeKey] = {
    version: runtimeVersion,
    modules: {
      effect: EffectNS,
      '@athrio/loom-lang-services/LanguageService': LanguageServiceNS,
    },
  } satisfies HostRuntime
}

